adjust their bids, offers, and negotiating positions, which could affect the market and harm SPS

and its customers.

           7.       The Protective Order further states:

                Within seven days of the filing of the request seeking confidential treatment of
                the material, or as otherwise ordered, Staff shall, and any intervenor may, file a
                response addressing the legal and factual merits of each confidentiality claim
                including Staff’s or the party’s position on whether the material is in fact
                confidential.2
           8.       As of seven days after the filing of SPS’s request, no intervenor in this case has

challenged the Request for Confidential Treatment of Exhibit BR 3-1 (CONF).

           9.       Upon review of the Request, the laws and rules governing confidential treatment of

non-public information provided pursuant to a Bench Request, and the procedures governing such

information provided for in the Protective Order, the Hearing Examiners determine that SPS has

satisfied its burden and that the information contained in Exhibit BR 3-1 (CONF) should be

afforded confidential treatment by the Commission and remain non-public.




